{
  "raw": {
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193621": {
      "title": "2027-28 Jennifer Jahrling Forese Writer-in-Residence: Journalism",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of English Location: Waterville, Maine Colby College’s Creative Writing Program invites applications for the 2027–2028 Jennifer Jahrling Forese Residency. We seek a journalist with experience in deep reportage, long-form and investigative storytelling, or digital, multimedia, and podcast journalism. This yearlong residency provides a dedicated space for a writer to develop a major project while engaging with a dynamic campus community eager to explore the literary craft and public inquiry needed to create multi-layered stories that ground us in our shared humanity. Our program sponsors a literary festival, a long-standing visiting writers’ series, community readings, the Kristina Stahl Writer-in-Residence Program, and the Jennifer Jahrling Forese Writer-in-Residence Program. Colby is a highly selective liberal arts college recognized for excellence in undergraduate education and close student-faculty interaction. We support students who edit two literary magazines, write for ECHO , Colby’s student newspaper, and engage actively in service learning and interdisciplinary partnerships. Our students benefit from an innovative curriculum that includes offerings such as short forms, documentary radio, and environmental writing. We are especially interested in candidates who, through their creative activity, teaching, and service, will promote inclusion and belonging at Colby. Duties include teaching two classes, one in the fall and one in the spring, designed by the resident in consultation with Creative Writing Program faculty; delivering one public presentation of work-in-progress and one public lecture or workshop for a general audience; scheduling office hours; working one-on-one with selected students; and engaging with the broader Waterville community. In addition to a generous honorarium, Colby provides a stipend for moving expenses. Qualifications: An MFA or terminal degree in a related field, at least one published book or a significant publication record, and experience teaching at the undergraduate level. To apply, submit your application through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193621. Include a cover letter, CV, a statement of teaching philosophy, a detailed description of the two proposed undergraduate courses, a writing sample of no more than twenty pages, and two letters of reference. The anticipated salary range for this position is $79,200 to $83,200, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Interviews for shortlisted candidates will be scheduled in late January 2027, and the selected candidate will be informed in early February 2027. For further inquiries about the application and information about the Jennifer Jahrling Forese Writer-in-Residence program, contact the director of the Creative Writing Program, Professor Arisa White, at aawhite@colby.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193968": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Position in Music Theory",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of Music Location: Waterville, Maine The Colby College Department of Music invites applications for a Tenure-Track Assistant Professor position in Music Theory to begin on July 1, 2027. We are seeking a dynamic teacher and scholar whose research specialization and course offerings will complement those of the existing department. The successful candidate will join a small, collegial department and will have a teaching load of 4.5 courses per academic year (alternating between 4 and 5 courses every other year) that will include: Music Theory I (MU181) Music Theory II (MU182) Electives (1–3 per academic year) Additional courses may include: an upper-level theory course; Senior Seminar; a first-year writing course (W1) The successful candidate will propose courses that are engaging, broadly appealing, and, for the electives, suitable for both majors and non-majors and will have a vision for Colby’s Music Theory curriculum that will generate excitement among students. The candidate will also maintain a strong research trajectory in their field(s) of specialization. The search committee is especially interested in candidates with a record of previous college teaching and who, through their research, teaching, and service, will contribute to access and belonging within the campus community. Preference will be given to candidates holding a Ph.D. in Music Theory at the time of appointment. To apply, please submit an application through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/193968. Include 1) a cover letter outlining interests and specific qualifications for the position; 2) CV; 3) three confidential letters of recommendation (uploadable directly to Interfolio); 4) titles and brief descriptions for 3 course offerings (2 electives, 1 upper-level theory course) and a brief description for Music Theory I; 5) a statement of teaching philosophy that demonstrates a commitment to equitable and inclusive teaching, with special consideration for the liberal arts college context; and 6) a representative example of recent scholarship. Review of applications will begin October 16, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. The anticipated salary range for this position is $92,700 to $96,700, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194052": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor position in Analytical Chemistry",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of Chemistry Location: Waterville, Me The Department of Chemistry at Colby College invites applications for a tenure-track, Assistant Professor position in analytical chemistry to begin fall 2027. We welcome applications from analytical chemists in all disciplines with preference for expertise in environmental applications, particularly in aqueous or terrestrial settings. The Colby Chemistry Department offers excellent instrumentation and computational resources to support analytical work. The College has a vibrant program in environmental sciences bridging multiple departments and partnerships with regional institutions such as Bigelow Laboratory for Ocean Sciences, The Seven Lakes Alliance, and Colby’s Island Campus. For more information about this position, please see: https://web.colby.edu/analytical/ . The position carries an average annual teaching load of 4.5 courses. The anticipated salary range for this position is $92,700 to $96,700, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors. Research is supported with a competitive startup package. Application review will begin October 9, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Candidates must have earned a Ph.D. in chemistry or a related field by the position start date, demonstrate a commitment to teaching undergraduate General Chemistry and Analytical chemistry, and be able to contribute to our environmental chemistry curriculum. Qualified candidates will also demonstrate a strong commitment to building an active research program involving undergraduate students. Applicants must also submit a current curriculum vitae, a statement of research interests, a statement of teaching philosophy, undergraduate and graduate transcripts, three confidential letters of recommendation, and two samples of current scholarship (e.g., a recent publication or manuscript) as PDFs to: https://apply.interfolio.com/194052 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194246": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor in Jewish Studies",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of Jewish Studies Location: Waterville, ME The Colby College Department of Jewish Studies seeks a one-year Visiting Assistant Professor for the 2027/28 academic year to teach three Modern Hebrew language courses (beginning through intermediate) and one course in Israel studies on a topic of their choosing. The successful candidate will be passionate about teaching introductory Hebrew in a fun, engaging, creative manner and committed to employing a multivocal approach when teaching about Israel. Candidates must have relevant prior teaching experience, a solid command of Modern Hebrew, and a master’s degree or higher (or equivalent advanced degree) at time of employment. (Colby will arrange for the successful candidate to receive training in the Ulpan-Or Hebrew language curriculum prior to the start of the fall semester.) The Jewish Studies department prioritizes outstanding pedagogy, a commitment to student mentorship beyond the classroom, and engagement in co-curricular activities. Colby College is proud to be among the best small liberal arts colleges for Jewish life and learning. To apply, please submit applications through Interfolio. Include a cover letter explaining your interest in this specific position, a cv, and two letters of recommendation. Please also submit a statement that describes your approach to pedagogy and how it will inform the courses you would teach at Colby; we encourage you to include specific examples that illustrate your use of active learning and student-centered teaching methods. Review of applications will begin on October 26, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. The anticipated salary range for this position is $65,700 to $69,700, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors. Colby is a private, coeducational liberal arts college that admits students and makes personnel decisions on the basis of the individual’s qualifications to contribute to Colby’s educational objectives and institutional needs. The principle of not discriminating on the basis of race, color, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, caste, national or ethnic origin, marital status, genetic information, political beliefs, veteran or military status, parental status, pregnancy, childbirth or related medical conditions, physical or mental disability unrelated to the job or course of study requirements is consistent with the mission of a liberal arts college and the law. Relevant prior teaching experience A solid command of Modern Hebrew A master’s degree or higher (or equivalent advanced degree) at time of employment To apply, please submit applications through Interfolio. Include a cover letter explaining your interest in this specific position, a cv, and two letters of recommendation. Please also submit a statement that describes your approach to pedagogy and how it will inform the courses you would teach at Colby; we encourage you to include specific examples that illustrate your use of active learning and student-centered teaching methods. Review of applications will begin on October 26, 2026, and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194259": {
      "title": "1-Year French Department Visiting Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of French and Italian Location: Waterville, ME Visiting Assistant Professor Position in French and Francophone Studies Colby College The Department of French and Italian Studies is seeking a Visiting Assistant Professor in Francophone Studies for a one-year position to begin July 1st, 2027. PhD expected. The successful candidate will be part of an energetic department committed to providing the best liberal arts education to talented undergraduates. We seek a teacher-scholar with extensive experience teaching across the curriculum and working with a diverse student body. The area of specialization is open; preference given to candidates specializing in the literatures and/or the cultures of the Francophone Americas, which include Quebec, North America and the French Caribbean, as well as, potentially, one or more of the following regions: the Indian Ocean, the Pacific Islands, North Africa, or the Middle East and their diasporas. We seek candidates whose research interests complement those already represented among the department’s French faculty while also expanding and diversifying our curricular offerings. Areas of research specialization may include interdisciplinary fields such as visual culture, cultural history, gender studies, ecocriticism, medical humanities, Atlantic studies, island studies, Indigenous studies and disability studies among others. This one-year Visiting Assistant position carries a teaching load of five courses at the beginning, intermediate, and advanced levels. Candidates must have a native or near-native fluency in French and English, as well as a strong commitment to teaching all levels of French language. Applicants should submit the following: cover letter describing how their research interests and teaching experience speak to the job description, curriculum vitae, three confidential letters of recommendation, a one-page statement of teaching philosophy, two full sets of course evaluations, and a representative sample of current scholarship (not to exceed 25 pages) via Interfolio at the following link, X . Review of applications will begin November 15th, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Address all inquiries to Valerie M. Dionne (vmdionne@colby.edu), Chair of French Search Committee. The anticipated salary range for this position is $82,800 to $86,800, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors. Colby is a private, coeducational liberal arts college that admits students and makes personnel decisions on the basis of the individual’s qualifications to contribute to Colby’s educational objectives and institutional needs. The principle of not discriminating on the basis of race, color, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, caste, national or ethnic origin, marital status, genetic information, political beliefs, veteran or military status, parental status, pregnancy, childbirth or related medical conditions, physical or mental disability unrelated to the job or course of study requirements is consistent with the mission of a liberal arts college and the law. PhD expected Applicants should submit the following: cover letter describing how their research interests and teaching experience speak to the job description, curriculum vitae, three confidential letters of recommendation, a one-page statement of teaching philosophy, two full sets of course evaluations, and a representative sample of current scholarship (not to exceed 25 pages) via Interfolio at the following link, X . Review of applications will begin November 15th, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Address all inquiries to Valerie M. Dionne (vmdionne@colby.edu), Chair of French Search Committee."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194271": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Macroeconomics",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of Economics Location: Waterville, ME We are searching for a macroeconomist with potential for excellence in research and teaching who can contribute to our macroeconomics curriculum, especially at the intermediate level. All department faculty also offer elective courses that are informed by their scholarship. The teaching load at Colby is 4.5 classes per year, typically achieved in the department through three distinct courses. We are seeking candidates from a wide variety of backgrounds who, through their teaching, scholarship, and service, will contribute to access, belonging, and inclusive teaching within the campus community. Colby is a selective liberal arts college located in Central Maine with high standards for teaching and research. Our 20 continuing department faculty have active research agendas generously supported by the College, including a pre-tenure sabbatical during the fourth year, significant start-up and continuing research funds, and department-funded student research and teaching assistants. Our review of completed applications will begin on November 13, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. We plan to conduct first-round interviews in mid-December and to schedule campus visits for finalists in January. To be considered for an interview, your application dossier must include: (i) a cover letter that describes your motivation for applying to Colby and outlines your research agenda; (ii) your curriculum vitae; (iii) three confidential letters of recommendation; (iv) a representative sample of your research; (v) your graduate transcript; (vi) a statement of teaching philosophy that demonstrates a commitment to undergraduate teaching in a liberal arts environment; (vii) and evidence of classroom teaching effectiveness, including course evaluations if available. Candidates should have completed a Ph.D. prior to September 1, 2027.  The anticipated salary range for this position is $119,800 to $123,800, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors. Please submit all materials electronically to: https://apply.interfolio.com/194271 Please direct any questions about the position to: econsearch@colby.edu Please visit www.colby.edu and http://www.colby.edu/economics/ for more information about the College and Department of Economics."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194957": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor (Japanese) EALC",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of East Asian Languages and Cultures Location: Waterville, ME The Department of East Asian Languages and Cultures at Colby College seeks applicants for a one-year visiting assistant professor position in Japanese during the 2027-2028 academic year. The successful applicant will teach four Japanese language classes, two in the fall semester and two in the spring semester, at a range of levels. The anticipated salary range for this position is $65,700 to $69,700, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors. Colby is a private, coeducational liberal arts college that admits students and makes personnel decisions on the basis of the individual’s qualifications to contribute to Colby’s educational objectives and institutional needs. The principle of not discriminating on the basis of race, color, age, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, religion, caste, national or ethnic origin, marital status, genetic information, political beliefs, veteran or military status, parental status, pregnancy, childbirth or related medical conditions, physical or mental disability unrelated to the job or course of study requirements is consistent with the mission of a liberal arts college and the law. A graduate degree in Japanese language, literature, or a related field Native or near-native proficiency in Japanese and English Prior language teaching experience A demonstrated commitment to fostering access and belonging within the campus community through teaching and engagement A master’s degree is required A PhD is preferred ABD candidates are encouraged to apply Please submit applications through Interfolio . Applications should include the following materials: (1) cover letter; (2) curriculum vitae; (3) three letters of recommendation; (4) teaching statement; (5) graduate transcript(s); (6) course evaluations. (If no course evaluations are available, please submit other evidence of teaching effectiveness, such as course syllabi or a short teaching video.) Review of applications will begin on November 2, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Any questions should be directed to Laura Nüffer, Assistant Professor of East Asian Languages and Cultures, at lknuffer@colby.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195065": {
      "title": "1 Year Visiting Professor - Global Studies Department",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of Global Studies Location: Waterville, ME The Global Studies Department at Colby College invites applications for a one-year position at the Visiting Assistant Professor rank, beginning July 1, 2027. We seek a transdisciplinary scholar of critical political economy, with teaching and research in such areas as postcolonial political economy, feminist political economy, cultural political economy, or critical development studies. Candidates should be prepared to teach courses in areas that could include: health, migration/borders, global supply chains, extraction, the commons, political ecology, displacement, empire, critical humanitarianism, among others. The search committee is especially interested in candidates who, through their research, teaching, and/or service, draw on and contribute to critical perspectives and pedagogies of the Global South, Indigenous, migrant, and other underrepresented communities. We welcome candidates whose scholarship centers alternative wisdom traditions, epistemologies, futurities, and often overlooked traditions of resistance. Global Studies is one of the largest interdisciplinary majors at Colby. As one of the oldest Global Studies departments at a liberal arts college, the department has a history of drawing on faculty and classes from across the college. Building on this foundation, Global Studies at Colby is in the midst of a multi-year process of aligning our curriculum with critical and transdisciplinary approaches to global studies. We are particularly excited to welcome a new colleague who might engage in, and contribute to, that process with us. Depending on training and interests, they could also participate in relevant centers and institutions on campus, including social science and humanities departments, interdisciplinary departments such as Latin American Studies, East Asian Languages and Cultures, Environmental Studies, Women's, Gender, and Sexuality Studies (WGSS), STS, and initiatives such as the Oak Institute for Human Rights, the Center for the Arts and Humanities, and the Critical Indigenous Studies Initiative (CISI). Given our department’s commitment to transdisciplinarity, the successful candidate will either be trained in a transdisciplinary field or trained in a discipline with demonstrated transdisciplinary teaching and research. We seek highly qualified candidates with demonstrated excellence in teaching who can make new and critical contributions to our concentrations within the Global Studies major in International Economic Policy (IEP), International Relations and Foreign Policy (IR/FP), and Global Health, among others. The position carries a teaching load of four classes per year, including GS101: Introduction to Global Studies as well as GS electives in the candidate’s area of expertise. The successful candidate will be a creative and engaged teacher, able to teach courses that emphasize interdisciplinary approaches, intersectional engagement, and anti- and/or de-colonial perspectives in ways that will complement the department’s current strengths and broaden our offerings. Applicants should submit the following materials via Interfolio cover letter curriculum vitae statement of teaching philosophy, which should demonstrate commitment to inclusive teaching evidence of teaching excellence, including course evaluations, if available, and at least one syllabus for an elective course that the candidate feels illustrates the transdisciplinary teaching that they would bring to the position Optional: one representative sample of recent scholarly writing Selected candidates will be asked to provide the names of three references Review of complete applications will begin on December 4 and continue until the position is filled. Questions about the position can be addressed to the Chair of Global Studies, Maple Razsa ( mjrazsa@colby.edu ) Questions about the application process can be directed to the Global Studies Admin, Tom Fortin ( tfortin@colby.edu )"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195282": {
      "title": "Non-Tenure Track Continuing Professor position in Piano Performance",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of Music Location: Waterville, Maine The Colby College Department of Music invites applications for a Non-Tenure Track Continuing Professor position in Piano Performance to begin on July 1, 2027. We are seeking a dynamic teacher and performer whose research and performance specialization and course offerings will complement those of the existing department. The successful candidate will join a small, collegial department, with responsibilities that include: Teaching undergraduate students in the piano studio and coach assigned chamber ensemble groups. Prepare students for recitals on and off campus. Prepare students for juries and organize piano workshops for students. Coordinating all piano studies, including: audition all new piano students each semester and make teacher assignments; attend and adjudicate recitals and juries for students of other piano instructors; adjudicate departmental auditions, juries and recitals; evaluate music supplements of prospective piano students; support recruitment of prospective students, assess piano maintenance/tuning needs and coordinate work with the piano technician. Teaching a class in the applicant’s field of performance/research that could be a synthesis of applied and academic study and that, for example, could be in fields such as chamber music, among others. The successful candidate will propose courses that are engaging, broadly-appealing for music majors, minors, and the wider student community at Colby. As a whole, the candidate will have a vision for Colby’s Piano performance and chamber music curriculum that will generate excitement among students. The candidate will also maintain a strong research and performance trajectory in their field(s) of specialization. The search committee is especially interested in candidates with a record of previous college teaching and who, through their research and teaching, will foster access and belonging throughout the campus community. The successful candidate has the potential to take on a leadership position in the department. Preference will be given to candidates who have earned  a DMA or a  Ph.D. in piano performance by the time of appointment. To apply, please submit applications through Interfolio at XXX . Include: 1) a cover letter outlining interests and specific qualifications for the position; 2) Curriculum Vitae; 3) three confidential letters of recommendation (uploadable directly to Interfolio); 4 ) titles and brief descriptions for two courses offerings; 5) a statement of teaching philosophy that dem onstrates a commitment to inclusive teaching; and, 6) A portfolio with links to three representative examples (videos are preferred) of recent performances. Review of applications will begin December 14, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Questions about the application or submission process should be directed to: José Martínez, Assistant Professor Department of Music, Colby College jose.martinez@colby.edu The anticipated salary range for this position is $82,800 to $86,800, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195206": {
      "title": "",
      "description": ""
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195833": {
      "title": "Economics VAP (2 year, Accounting)",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of Economics Location: Colby College Posted: Oct 16, 2026 The Economics Department at Colby College invites applications for a 2-year visiting position beginning in the 2027-28 academic year. The teaching expectations for this position will be five courses/sections per year, including offering at least three sections of Accounting and Financial Analysis, our prerequisite accounting course for students taking corporate finance and majoring in our Economics and Financial Markets concentration. The remaining two courses may be fulfilled by teaching corporate finance and/or related electives. We are seeking candidates from a wide variety of backgrounds who will contribute to access, belonging, and inclusive teaching within the campus community. Successful candidates must demonstrate their potential for excellence in teaching. Colby is a selective liberal arts college located in Central Maine with high standards for teaching. Our students are motivated, intellectually curious, and easily engaged in the classroom and in independent research. Our 20 continuing department faculty have active research agendas generously supported by the College and teaching support from the College’s Center for Teaching and Learning. This visiting position includes a stipend for transitioning to Central Maine and funding for conference travel. The appointment will be made at the rank of Visiting Assistant Professor or Visiting Instructor, depending on the successful candidate’s qualifications. The anticipated salary range for this position is $82,600 to $90,300, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors. Our review of completed applications will begin on December 1  and continue until the position is filled. We will conduct remote interviews via electronic conferencing in filling this position. To be considered for an interview, your application dossier must include: (i) a cover letter that describes your motivation for applying to Colby; (ii) your resume or curriculum vitae; (iii) evidence of classroom teaching effectiveness, including course evaluations if available; (iv) a statement of teaching philosophy that demonstrates a commitment to undergraduate teaching in a liberal arts environment; and (v) three confidential letters of recommendation, at least one of which must address your potential for teaching excellence. Candidates certified as a CPA, CFA, and/or have a master’s degree, PhD, or ABD in accounting, economics, and/or financial analysis are welcome to apply. Please submit all materials electronically to: https://apply.interfolio.com/195833"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195912": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor of Performance, Theater, and Dance (Design)",
      "description": "Department: Colby College: Department of Performance, Theater, and Dance Location: Waterville, ME Posted: Oct 19, 2026 The Colby College Department of Performance, Theater, and Dance seeks a one-year Visiting Assistant Professor in Design for the 2027/28 academic year, appointment beginning July 1, 2027; to teach two courses (Introduction to Design and an additional course in the candidate’s area of specialization) and create two designs for department productions (flexible design area). The successful candidate will be sincerely interested in joining a liberal arts teaching environment, committed to student mentorship, and keen to collaborate within a department that champions innovative and interdisciplinary performance practices and new work development. Candidates must have expertise in scenic design and/or lighting design, and hold an MFA degree or equivalent professional experience at the time of hire. Expertise in a secondary design or research area is desirable; this might include projection design, sound design, virtual worlds, computational methods, etc. Relevant design expertise Teaching/mentorship experience An MFA in design or a related field, or equivalent professional experience To apply, please submit applications through Interfolio [https://apply.interfolio.com/195912]. Include a cover letter explaining your interest in this specific position, a cv, a design portfolio (links to digital portfolios are preferred) and contact information for three professional references. Please also submit a statement that describes your approach to design pedagogy and how it will inform the courses you would teach at Colby; we encourage you to include specific examples that illustrate your ability to mentor students and to teach inclusively across a range of student experience levels. Review of applications will begin on December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Questions may be directed to the search chair, Rachel Anderson-Rabern, at raanders@colby.edu The anticipated salary range for this position is $65,700 to $69,700, commensurate with rank, experience, qualifications, and other relevant factors."
    }
  },
